Subject: [PATCH 10571/11362] Record what commit 255035dfdb actually contains,
 and why hooks never saw it

That commit carries a conductor checkpoint message but its content is the fourth
QA-layer widening (REQ-VERIFY-6802, SCENARIO-VERIFY-6802-D): _has_compute_bound_marker
now recognises an artifact that claims a training run in its own title or verdict while
naming no model, framework, or runner. That gap let experiment_746 declare
"dualgpu_retrain_validated" in 1.73 seconds and pass the fabrication gate.

The claim is read ONLY from the artifact's own self-describing fields, never from the
whole blob. A retrospective that lists a training experiment is not a training run, and
a whole-blob scan made 13 retros and closeout artifacts compute-bound. An honest
blocked_* verdict is excluded too: it reports that nothing ran, so flagging it would
punish exactly the honesty the Pre-Launch Preconditions Discipline asks for.

The token list holds one word. "dualgpu" and "gpu" were measured and rejected -- they
name infrastructure work (harness patches, enforcement audits, zombie detection) as
often as a real run.

Blast radius measured before landing, and again against the committed module: 16
artifacts newly critical across all 5,915, every one a retrain claiming completion in
1.7 to 31 seconds; 0 flags lost. Those 16 are NOT stamped here. Stamping historical
records is a separate backfill decision that writes to evidence.

5 of 5 mutations RED, source restored byte-identical. The 7 failures and 4 errors in the
adjacent suite reproduce identically on the pre-change file, so they are not from this;
one "error" is a teardown memory watchdog on a test that passes.

Why the misleading message: the conductor's checkpoint path stages every dirty file
straight from git diff and never consults claimed_by_other_sessions, so a declared scope
binds the normal commit path and nothing else. It also commits with verification
disabled, so no hook ran on the change. Hooks were run by hand afterwards and pass,
including spec coverage, ruff and mypy. Filed in known-issues with a proposal to run
hooks after a checkpoint and REPORT rather than refuse -- refusing would restore the
data loss that disabling verification was added to prevent.

A mutation marker was deleted deliberately to land this. It was armed by a concurrent
run of test_experiment_6833's suite, and its only path was this file -- my own authored
edit, caught inside that run's window. The marker recorded attributed_to_run: [] with
write_observation: "recorded", meaning the observation worked and that test wrote nothing.
So the marker held no genuine signal. This is the documented over-report: the tool diffs a
shared working tree and cannot tell whose write it was. Nothing was reverted.

